/**
 * @page py_backends Simulation Backends
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section py_backends_overview Overview
 *
 * Maestro provides a uniform interface to multiple quantum simulation algorithms and engines.
 * You can explicitly select the simulator backend and method via `SimulatorConfig`.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_statevector Statevector Simulation
 *
 * Full 2^N amplitude statevector simulation. Recommended for high entanglement circuits with up to 30 qubits on CPU or 36+ on GPU.
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.QCSim,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.Statevector,
 * )
 * result = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, config=config, shots=2000)
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_mps Matrix Product State (MPS)
 *
 * MPS enables simulation of circuits with hundreds of qubits when entanglement is bounded (1D area law).
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * mps_config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.QCSim,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.MatrixProductState,
 *     max_bond_dimension=64,
 *     singular_value_threshold=1e-10,
 *     lookahead_depth=3,
 *     disable_optimized_swapping=False,
 * )
 *
 * result = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, config=mps_config, shots=1000)
 * print(result["method"])  # "MatrixProductState"
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection py_mps_truncation_mode Truncation Mode
 *
 * `singular_value_threshold` is interpreted differently depending on `truncation_mode`:
 *
 * - `"discarded_weight"` (the default on every backend): discard the smallest singular
 *   values until their cumulative squared weight, normalized by the total, would reach
 *   the threshold. This matches Qiskit Aer's and ITensor's convention, so it's the mode
 *   to use when comparing bond-dimension growth against an external ITensor run.
 * - `"relative_max"`: discard singular values below `threshold * sigma_max` (the
 *   largest singular value at that bond). This was the historical default for the
 *   QCSim and GPU backends before `discarded_weight` became the default everywhere.
 *
 * Only the QCSim and GPU backends support `"relative_max"`; the Aer backend always
 * implements `"discarded_weight"` and raises `ValueError` if `"relative_max"` is
 * requested.
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * # Opt back into the pre-existing QCSim/GPU convention
 * mps_config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.QCSim,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.MatrixProductState,
 *     singular_value_threshold=1e-10,
 *     truncation_mode="relative_max",
 * )
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection py_mps_gpu_svd GPU SVD Algorithm Selection
 *
 * For GPU MPS, MPO, and Tensor Network simulations, you can select the underlying cuSOLVER SVD algorithm
 * to balance precision against execution speed:
 *
 * `mps_svd_solver`, `mpo_svd_solver` and `tensor_network_svd_solver` each take one of:
 *
 * - `"gesvd"`: Standard QR-based SVD, the current GPU plugin default.
 * - `"gesvdj"`: Jacobi-based SVD, optimal for small to medium bond dimensions.
 * - `"gesvdp"`: Polar-decomposition SVD.
 * - `"gesvdr"`: Randomized SVD for fixed-rank truncation.
 *   Tensor Network execution falls back to GESVD when this is selected.
 *
 * The default `None` leaves the choice to the installed GPU plugin (currently GESVD
 * for all three).
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * gpu_config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.Gpu,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.MatrixProductState,
 *     mps_svd_solver="gesvdj",
 * )
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_stabilizer Stabilizer (Clifford-only)
 *
 * Ultra-fast polynomial-time simulation for circuits comprising only Clifford gates (H, S, CX, CZ, X, Y, Z). Scales to thousands of qubits.
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * clifford_qasm = """
 * OPENQASM 2.0;
 * include "qelib1.inc";
 * qreg q[100];
 * creg c[100];
 * h q[0];
 * cx q[0], q[1];
 * cx q[1], q[2];
 * measure q -> c;
 * """
 *
 * stab_config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.QCSim,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.Stabilizer,
 * )
 * result = maestro.simple_execute(clifford_qasm, config=stab_config, shots=10000)
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_backend_table Backend Compatibility Matrix
 *
 * | SimulationType | QCSim | Qiskit Aer | GPU | DistGpu | DistMpiGpu | QuEST |
 * |----------------|-------|------------|-----|---------|------------|-------|
 * | `Statevector` | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
 * | `MatrixProductState` | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | No | No |
 * | `Stabilizer` | Yes | Yes | No | No | No | No |
 * | `TensorNetwork` | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | No | No |
 * | `PauliPropagator` | Yes | No | Yes | No | No | No |
 * | `ExtendedStabilizer` | No | Yes | No | No | No | No |
 *
 * **Distributed & Partitioned Execution:**
 * - `DistributedGpu`: Statevector distributed across local GPUs in a single node via `libmaestro_gpu_distributed.so`.
 * - `DistributedMpiGpu`: Statevector distributed across MPI ranks and GPUs (single or multi-node) via `libmaestro_gpu_distributed_mpi.so` and CUDA-aware MPI.
 * - `CompositeQCSim` / `CompositeQiskitAer`: CPU-based p-block composite simulation for partitioned circuits (not MPI-based).
 * - `QuestSim`: QuEST engine wrapper for MPI-distributed statevector simulation across cluster nodes.
 *
 * See @ref py_hpc for setup, configuration, and execution examples for distributed GPU and MPI workloads.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_advanced Object-Oriented Simulator Control
 *
 * For full low-level control over the simulation lifecycle, use the `Maestro` engine directly:
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * import maestro
 *
 * m = maestro.Maestro()
 *
 * # Create a simulator handle
 * handle = m.create_simulator(
 *     maestro.SimulatorType.QCSim,
 *     maestro.SimulationType.Statevector
 * )
 *
 * # Get the simulator object and prepare a Bell state
 * sim = m.get_simulator(handle)
 * sim.AllocateQubits(2)
 * sim.InitializeSimulator()
 * sim.ApplyH(0)
 * sim.ApplyCX(0, 1)
 * print(sim.SampleCounts([0, 1], shots=1024))  # {0: ..., 3: ...}
 *
 * # Clean up when finished
 * m.destroy_simulator(handle)
 * @endcode
 *
 * These methods operate directly on the selected simulator, without the
 * single-host network used by `simple_execute` and `QuantumCircuit.execute`.
 * Use the returned object only while its handle is alive; do not call its
 * methods after `destroy_simulator(handle)`.
 *
 * `Simulator` exposes the low-level operations from `maestrolib/Interface.h`:
 *
 * | Category | Methods |
 * |----------|---------|
 * | Lifecycle | `AllocateQubits`, `GetNumberOfQubits`, `InitializeSimulator`, `ResetSimulator`, `ClearSimulator`, `FlushSimulator` |
 * | Configuration | `ConfigureSimulator`, `GetConfiguration`, `GetSimulatorType`, `GetSimulationType`, `SetMultithreading`, `GetMultithreading`, `IsQcsim` |
 * | Measurement and state queries | `Measure`, `MeasureNoCollapse`, `ApplyReset`, `Probability`, `Amplitude`, `AllProbabilities`, `Probabilities`, `SampleCounts` |
 * | Saved state | `SaveState`, `RestoreState`, `SaveStateToInternalDestructive`, `RestoreInternalDestructiveSavedState` |
 * | Single-qubit gates | `ApplyX`, `ApplyY`, `ApplyZ`, `ApplyH`, `ApplyS`, `ApplySDG`, `ApplyT`, `ApplyTDG`, `ApplySX`, `ApplySXDG`, `ApplyK`, `ApplyP`, `ApplyRx`, `ApplyRy`, `ApplyRz`, `ApplyU` |
 * | Controlled gates | `ApplyCX`, `ApplyCY`, `ApplyCZ`, `ApplyCH`, `ApplyCSX`, `ApplyCSXDG`, `ApplyCP`, `ApplyCRx`, `ApplyCRy`, `ApplyCRz`, `ApplyCU`, `ApplyCCX` |
 * | Swaps | `ApplySwap`, `ApplyCSwap` |
 *
 * `Initialize`, `Reset`, `Configure`, `Clear`, and `Flush` are shorter aliases
 * for the corresponding methods ending in `Simulator`. Existing `set_seed`
 * and density-matrix diagnostic methods remain available.
 *
 * Pass Python sequences for qubits or outcomes; array lengths are inferred.
 * `Amplitude` returns a complex number, `AllProbabilities` and `Probabilities`
 * return lists, and `SampleCounts` returns a dictionary of integer outcomes
 * to counts (`shots=1000` by default). Python owns these results, so the C API's
 * memory-release functions are unnecessary. `Probabilities` takes basis-state
 * indices, whereas `Measure`, `ApplyReset`, and `SampleCounts` take qubit indices.
 * Packed measurement outcomes use the first listed qubit as the least-significant
 * bit. For `MeasureNoCollapse`, qubit 0 is the least-significant bit.
 *
 * Type queries return `SimulatorType` and `SimulationType` enum values; flag
 * queries return booleans. Operations with no result return `None` and native
 * exceptions propagate as Python exceptions. `ApplyU` and `ApplyCU` accept
 * `theta`, `phi`, `lambda_`, and optional `gamma=0.0`.
 *
 * Backend-specific restrictions still apply. In particular, the destructive
 * save/restore pair supports internal-state sampling for Aer: flush pending
 * gates, save, call `MeasureNoCollapse`, then restore before applying more gates.
 */
